  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Kofi Awoonor was a Ghanaian poet, novelist, scholar and diplomat, born in the Volta Region in 1935.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Rediscovery and Other Poems appeared in 1964, in the first years of Ghanaian independence.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    His grandmother was a dirge singer, and the rhythms and repetitions of Ewe oral poetry shape his line.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Stanza one is ten lines; stanza two is twelve.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    There is no fixed metre or rhyme; the poem is organised by two extended metaphors and by the prayer that begins stanza two.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    "Caught between the anvil and the hammer" places him between the tools, where the metal is; the pangs are being transformed in him.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A forge needs both tools; the persona is not under attack but under manufacture.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A forge is a place of making; the new life is being produced, and the question is what happens to the old metal.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    "Delivered" is a birth word, and the songs are the art made out of the pain; the poem is about making a person and making a poem.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The trapping is the inherited culture, described as tender and tenuous — precious and fragile at once.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Sisal is a plant fibre used for rope; the roughness is the texture of tradition, not a criticism of it.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The line names traditional religion precisely and without apology, which is part of the poem's recovery of the past.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    "Glories" praises and "flimsy" judges; the phrase is a mixed judgment, which is why it is so often examined.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Jargon is the poem's dismissive word for the new intellectual fashion; it sounds sophisticated and says little.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The search is "perpetual" and conducted on the outlaw's hill — risky, ongoing and outside the settled order.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    "Sew the old days for us, our fathers" turns description into request and makes the ancestors agents.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The preposition is the poem's politics: hidden, supporting, held against the skin.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Many rivers meet in one estuary; the image prepares for the sea at the end, where all the songs are held.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The stanza is honest about neglect and about survival at once: the old survives in fragments, inside the new anthems.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The new flags and anthems are made from snatches of the old tunes, which is the poem's model of syncretism.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The sea receives the rivers and holds their sound; nothing is lost in the mixture.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The past is to be sewn under the new garment: kept, hidden and supporting, not displayed instead of the present.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    "Flimsy glories" accepts the glamour and names the thinness, which is neither celebration nor refusal.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Syncretism is the fusion of two traditions into one living culture, and the two-layer garment is its image.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The prayer is made to those who hold continuity, and the thing asked for is a domestic craft — sewing — not a miracle.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Needle, sewing and garment take over from anvil, hammer and metal; the two systems are joined by the idea of transformation.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The repeated t gives the old culture a soft, deliberate texture, in contrast with the harder sound of "jargon".
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Stanza one describes what is happening in the present; stanza two begins with an imperative and asks for a resolution.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The speaker is one person speaking for a generation; the shift widens the poem's scope without changing its voice.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The tools are two halves of one process, and the persona is the material being made.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    "Under" is the key: the old is neither discarded nor displayed but worn against the body, supporting the new.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The sea holds all the rivers' songs; the mixture is not a loss but a container.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The goat's blood in the fetish hut is named precisely, without defence or embarrassment, which is itself a cultural claim.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The poem names no administrator and no policy; its subject is culture, art and identity, not colonial government.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The songs reverberate in the sea, but they are made of snatches and the people ignore what they inherit; the affirmation is qualified.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The pangs are transformed into songs, which is the poet's own work; the forge is an image of making art as well as a nation.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Both poems describe a power that shapes the people from outside; the difference is that Awoonor's forge is also a workshop of renewal.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    That sentence holds the sewing, the garment and the sea together, which is what a strong answer does.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    "Perpetual" and "outlaw" together make the search unfinished and dangerous, which qualifies any simple celebration of the new.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    "Under" carries the poem's whole politics of retention: hidden, supporting, in contact with the body.
  ]

]

